WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.928
A Python application should know which environment variables are required,
optional, sensitive, and dangerous to trust blindly.

2
00:00:06.928 --> 00:00:14.044
Non-secret configuration might identify the deployment environment, choose
a region, set a timeout, or enable a controlled feature.

3
00:00:14.044 --> 00:00:21.034
Sensitive configuration may include credentials, tokens, signing material,
database access details, or private key references.

4
00:00:21.034 --> 00:00:27.214
Mixing these categories makes it harder to apply the right access
controls, logging rules, and rotation procedures.

5
00:00:27.214 --> 00:00:29.898
Trust boundaries depend on who controls the value.

6
00:00:29.898 --> 00:00:36.888
Operator-controlled variables may be set by deployment tooling, secret
stores, infrastructure configuration, or CI/CD systems.

7
00:00:36.888 --> 00:00:43.629
User-controlled values may flow indirectly through job parameters,
notebooks, generated configuration, or stored records.

8
00:00:43.629 --> 00:00:47.998
A value that arrives in an environment variable is not automatically
trustworthy.

9
00:00:47.998 --> 00:00:53.054
Teams should understand the source, precedence, and approval path for each
important setting.

10
00:00:53.054 --> 00:00:56.799
Configuration sources and precedence can become security-relevant.

11
00:00:56.799 --> 00:01:04.664
A local file, process environment, deployment platform, secret store,
command-line option, or framework default may all compete to define a
value.

12
00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:12.653
If precedence is unclear, a development setting can override a production
expectation, or a missing secret can silently fall back to an unsafe
default.

13
00:01:12.653 --> 00:01:17.147
Configuration behavior should be visible enough that reviewers can reason
about it.

14
00:01:17.147 --> 00:01:21.329
Secret values should be minimized and separated from ordinary
configuration.

15
00:01:21.329 --> 00:01:32.314
A secret manager or managed platform secret feature may be appropriate for
sensitive values, while environment variables may carry references or
short-lived injected values depending on the architecture.

16
00:01:32.314 --> 00:01:41.240
The stable principle is to reduce how many places secrets live, limit who
can set or read them, and make it easy to rotate them without guessing
which systems depend on them.
